notes.body:
@alagoutte, I have solved Windows compile problem (yuck, it does not have designated initializers) and refactored identify payload dissect. Since I am going to complete dissection of entire payload, the function was getting too long, and I tried to automate the tree population using a structure definition. The next in turn is various GetLogPage payloads, and I am planning to reuse that code there.

To fix Windows compile, I have defined the -1,... list macros. I have checked the code   -- lots of places with -1,... long lists -- quite easy to make a mistake in the number of -1 -- all because of Windows compiler support. So, I thought it would be nice to export the MACRO definitions to header, and make all those places to use a MACRO.

What do you think?

@alagoutte,@AndersBroman, so yes, it was easier for me and the goal was to make it easier for everyone.

I am not here to change how you guys think, but to help with NVME parser. So, have it your way -- I will "unroll" the code.

I just want to know if both  of you object in principle on any "group" code -- for example, I added bitmask decoding via an unrolled loop. Or even there you want the "same" line repeated 8, 15 or 32 times? Let me know, so that I can complete the code and have it approved.

I mean the following pattern (preferably also adding the group automatically, but the code does not show that for brevity):
```
int fmask[16] { NEG_LST_16 };
....
{ &fmask[0], { "Name0", "path.f0", FT_UINT8, BASE_HEX, NULL, 0x1, NULL, HFILL} },
{ &fmask[1], { "Name1", "path.f1", FT_UINT8, BASE_HEX, NULL, 0x2, NULL, HFILL} },
.......

for ( i = 0; i < array_len(fmask); i++)
     proto_tree_add_item(grp,fmask[i], tvb, offset, 2, ENC_LITTLE_ENDIAN);
```

